WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.009
SPEAKER: Are your
applications deployed

00:00:02.009 --> 00:00:04.500
in hybrid and
multi-cloud environments?

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:07.260
Anthos can help you
modernize your existing apps,

00:00:07.260 --> 00:00:10.680
building new ones, and run
them anywhere securely.

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:14.200
Stay tuned to learn how to
get started with Anthos.

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:17.140
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:21.070 --> 00:00:23.300
Welcome to Anthos 101.

00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:25.550
Today, we will show
you how easy it

00:00:25.550 --> 00:00:27.680
is to get started with Anthos.

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:31.160
Let's create our first
Anthos deployment.

00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:32.960
In the Google Cloud
console, select

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:36.080
a project you want to work
in, or create a new one.

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:39.500
In the left navigation
menu, select Anthos.

00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:40.880
Enable the API.

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:42.990
This may take a few
seconds, but when done,

00:00:42.990 --> 00:00:45.260
you will see the
main landing page.

00:00:45.260 --> 00:00:48.410
In the Getting Started section,
you can read the documentation,

00:00:48.410 --> 00:00:50.510
create and register
your clusters,

00:00:50.510 --> 00:00:53.120
and launch Anthos
sample deployment.

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:57.050
This is what we are going to
do today, so click and launch.

00:00:57.050 --> 00:00:59.720
Now on the deployment
configuration screen,

00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:02.310
you can select an
existing service account,

00:01:02.310 --> 00:01:04.069
but since this is a
demo, we will just

00:01:04.069 --> 00:01:06.650
create a new service account.

00:01:06.650 --> 00:01:09.620
Leave the service account
name and ID as defaults,

00:01:09.620 --> 00:01:11.820
and add a description.

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:13.340
And click Deploy.

00:01:13.340 --> 00:01:15.290
While the deployment
is progressing,

00:01:15.290 --> 00:01:19.190
the cloud console transitions
to the Deployment Manager view,

00:01:19.190 --> 00:01:22.710
and after the sample is
deployed, in a few minutes,

00:01:22.710 --> 00:01:25.280
you will see a green
check mark and can

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:27.810
review the full deployment.

00:01:27.810 --> 00:01:30.880
You should see a list of
all enabled resources,

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:35.310
including the two Anthos GKE
clusters for prod and non-prod

00:01:35.310 --> 00:01:39.230
and one compute engine
instance for the jump server.

00:01:39.230 --> 00:01:40.070
And that's it.

00:01:40.070 --> 00:01:43.040
You just deployed a
sample app in Anthos.

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:44.990
When you go back to
the Anthos dashboard,

00:01:44.990 --> 00:01:48.530
you see service mesh card
and cluster status card.

00:01:48.530 --> 00:01:50.540
Cluster status is
green, indicating

00:01:50.540 --> 00:01:52.370
that the clusters are healthy.

00:01:52.370 --> 00:01:55.160
You can click on each cluster
and know more about them

00:01:55.160 --> 00:01:57.770
by navigating to Anthos GKE.

00:01:57.770 --> 00:02:00.200
Within the clusters,
we can see the nodes,

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:02.800
and inside each node,
we can see the pod

00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:05.090
summary and the details.

00:02:05.090 --> 00:02:07.790
In the workload section,
you can see workloads

00:02:07.790 --> 00:02:09.740
across all clusters.

00:02:09.740 --> 00:02:11.690
Under services and
ingress section,

00:02:11.690 --> 00:02:14.910
we have the Istio ingress
gateway service for the Anthos

00:02:14.910 --> 00:02:16.550
sample cluster 1.

00:02:16.550 --> 00:02:19.070
And ingress gateway
manages inbound traffic

00:02:19.070 --> 00:02:21.140
for your application
service mesh.

00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:24.500
And if we click on the external
endpoint using port 80,

00:02:24.500 --> 00:02:28.370
we see our deployed
application in production.

00:02:28.370 --> 00:02:30.530
Now, if we switch
back to the Anthos

00:02:30.530 --> 00:02:32.760
and click on the
service mesh card,

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:35.320
we see all of our services
in the table view.

00:02:35.320 --> 00:02:37.940
We can also switch
to topology graph.

00:02:37.940 --> 00:02:39.740
Anthos service
mesh automatically

00:02:39.740 --> 00:02:42.980
observes which services are
communicating with each other

00:02:42.980 --> 00:02:45.830
to show service-to-service
connection details.

00:02:45.830 --> 00:02:49.370
You can even drag and zoom
or hover over services

00:02:49.370 --> 00:02:53.690
to see additional details,
including outbound QPS.

00:02:53.690 --> 00:02:56.930
You can click on any
service to create SLOs.

00:02:56.930 --> 00:03:00.260
Let's say we want to create
an availability SLO where

00:03:00.260 --> 00:03:04.460
our shopping cart service is
99% available during a month.

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:07.860
There.

00:03:07.860 --> 00:03:10.970
Our SLO is set up.

00:03:10.970 --> 00:03:13.850
So we just deployed an
e-commerce application

00:03:13.850 --> 00:03:16.350
using Anthos sample deployment.

00:03:16.350 --> 00:03:18.290
Let's quickly review
the different components

00:03:18.290 --> 00:03:20.370
that we have deployed.

00:03:20.370 --> 00:03:22.680
There are two
Anthos GKE clusters

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:25.740
running on Google Cloud,
Anthos sample cluster 1

00:03:25.740 --> 00:03:28.980
and sample cluster 2,
and the Anthos service

00:03:28.980 --> 00:03:31.860
mesh deployed as separate
service meshes running

00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:33.540
on both clusters.

00:03:33.540 --> 00:03:36.900
We also have separate instances
of the online boutique

00:03:36.900 --> 00:03:40.270
application deployed and
running on both clusters.

00:03:40.270 --> 00:03:41.910
This is a web-based
e-commerce app

00:03:41.910 --> 00:03:44.340
that uses a number of
micro-services written

00:03:44.340 --> 00:03:46.240
in various
programming languages,

00:03:46.240 --> 00:03:49.860
including Java, Go,
Python, and JavaScript.

00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:53.400
And we had a Compute Engine
instance used as a jump server

00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:56.880
for accessing resources in
our project in both clusters

00:03:56.880 --> 00:03:59.440
and applying those changes.

00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:00.630
What are you waiting for?

00:04:00.630 --> 00:04:04.320
Start your Anthos journey today
using sample deployment as well

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:06.330
as the resources
linked below, and let

00:04:06.330 --> 00:04:08.970
us know how it goes in
the comments section.

00:04:08.970 --> 00:04:11.170
And for more Anthos and
Google Cloud content,

00:04:11.170 --> 00:04:13.450
don't forget to like and
subscribe to our channel.

00:04:13.450 --> 00:04:16.500
[MUSIC PLAYING]

